<p>Webinars are one of the most effective lead generation tools a small business can use. You get a room full of people who have already told you they are interested in what you do, you spend 30 to 60 minutes showing them you know your stuff, and at the end you invite them to take the next step. No cold calls. No chasing. Just a warm audience that already trusts you.</p>

<p>But most small businesses get webinars wrong. They pick a vague topic, throw up a registration page, send one email, and wonder why only four people show up. A webinar is not a single event. It is a funnel, and like any <a href="/blog/sales-funnel-small-business">sales funnel</a>, every stage needs to be built with intention.</p>

<p>This guide walks you through the full lifecycle. Choosing a topic that attracts the right audience, building a registration page that converts, sending a reminder sequence that maximises attendance, following up after the event, offering replays, and turning attendees into paying customers.</p>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>Step 1: Choose a topic people actually want to attend</h2>

<p>The biggest mistake with webinars is picking a topic that is too broad or too self-promotional. "An introduction to our services" is not a webinar topic. It is a sales pitch dressed up as education, and people can smell it a mile away.</p>

<p>A good webinar topic solves a specific, urgent problem that your ideal customer is already thinking about. It should be narrow enough to be useful in 30 to 45 minutes but important enough that someone will block out time in their calendar to learn about it.</p>

<p>Here are some examples of weak topics turned into strong ones:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Weak:</strong> "Digital marketing for small business." <strong>Strong:</strong> "Three email sequences every service business should be running right now."</li>
  <li><strong>Weak:</strong> "How we can help you grow." <strong>Strong:</strong> "How to get 50 Google reviews in 90 days without begging for them."</li>
  <li><strong>Weak:</strong> "Our product demo." <strong>Strong:</strong> "Build a follow-up system in 30 minutes that converts leads while you sleep."</li>
</ul>

<p>Notice the pattern. The strong topics promise a specific outcome in a specific timeframe. They focus on the audience, not on you. They answer the question every potential attendee is silently asking: "What will I walk away with?"</p>

<p>If you are not sure what to teach, look at the questions your customers ask you most often. Look at the problems they had before they found you. Those recurring conversations are your best content ideas, because you already know the answers.</p>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>Step 2: Build a registration page that fills seats</h2>

<p>Your registration page is a <a href="/blog/landing-pages-that-convert">landing page with one job</a>: convince visitors to sign up. It does not need to be complicated, but it does need to do a few things well.</p>

<p><strong>Lead with the outcome, not the format.</strong> Nobody wakes up thinking, "I would love to attend a webinar today." They wake up thinking about their problems. Your headline should speak to the problem you are solving and the result they will get. "Free Webinar" can go in a subheading. The headline itself should be the reason they care.</p>

<p><strong>Include a short bullet list of what they will learn.</strong> Three to five bullet points that describe the specific takeaways. Not vague promises. Concrete deliverables. "You will leave with a five-email follow-up sequence you can copy and use immediately" is far more compelling than "learn about email marketing."</p>

<p><strong>Add social proof if you have it.</strong> Testimonials from past attendees, the number of people who have already registered, or your own credibility markers (years in business, number of clients served, certifications). Anything that tells a visitor, "This person knows what they are talking about."</p>

<p><strong>Keep the form short.</strong> Name and email are enough. If you want a phone number for SMS reminders, you can ask for it, but every additional field reduces your registration rate. Collect what you need, nothing more.</p>

<blockquote><p>A webinar registration page is a landing page with one job. Everything on the page should push toward one action: sign up.</p></blockquote>

<p>Set a clear date and time, and display it prominently with the timezone. If you serve customers across multiple regions, consider mentioning the time in two or three zones. Nothing kills trust faster than someone showing up an hour late because the timezone was unclear.</p>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>Step 3: Send a reminder sequence that maximises attendance</h2>

<p>Here is a number that surprises most people: on average, only 30 to 40 percent of webinar registrants actually show up live. That means if 100 people register, you might get 35 in the room. The rest forgot, got busy, or lost the link.</p>

<p>A well-built reminder sequence closes that gap. It keeps your webinar top of mind and makes it easy for registrants to show up when the time comes. This is where <a href="/blog/marketing-automation-getting-started">marketing automation</a> becomes essential, because you cannot manually send five emails to every registrant and still run your business.</p>

<p>Here is the reminder sequence that works:</p>

<p><strong>Immediately after registration:</strong> Send a confirmation email with the date, time, timezone, and a link to add the event to their calendar. Include a brief recap of what they will learn. This email should feel welcoming, not transactional.</p>

<p><strong>Seven days before:</strong> Send a "looking forward to seeing you" email. Restate the value they will get. Share a quick tip or insight related to the topic to build anticipation. If you have a related blog post or resource, link to it.</p>

<p><strong>One day before:</strong> Send a reminder with the subject line focused on tomorrow. Keep it short. Restate the time and timezone. Include the join link. This is also a good time to send an SMS reminder if you collected phone numbers.</p>

<p><strong>One hour before:</strong> A short, punchy email. "We are live in one hour. Here is your link." That is all it needs to be. This email alone can lift your attendance rate by 10 to 15 percent.</p>

<p><strong>Live now:</strong> The moment the webinar starts, send a final email and SMS. "We are live. Join now." Include the link. Make it impossible to miss.</p>

<blockquote><p>Most people who register for your webinar want to attend. They just need you to make it easy by reminding them at the right moments.</p></blockquote>

<p>Each of these messages should be automated and triggered by the registration event. Set them up once, and every registrant gets the same sequence regardless of when they sign up. If someone registers three days before the event, they skip the seven-day reminder and pick up at the one-day mark. A good <a href="/blog/email-marketing-small-business">email marketing system</a> handles this logic automatically.</p>

